1470 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Among the top 25 nickel mines  causing deforestation, many operate in regions classified as High Carbon Stock \(HCS\) forests. Six of these mines contain over 75% HCS forests, which are rich in carbon and intact ecosystems, while 14 consist of 50% or less, indicating degraded lands \(Brown & Harris, 2024\). These areas, which store significant amounts of carbon, face substantial risks due to nickel-driven deforestation, potentially releasing millions of metric tons of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. For instance, deforestation within nickel concessions on Halmahera alone has resulted in an estimated loss of 2.04 million metric tons of stored carbon \(CRI, 2024\).  Preliminary findings suggest that regions with high GDP growth driven by mining activities may exhibit a significant positive forest carbon flux, indicating a reduced capacity of forests to absorb carbon \(Figure 2.18\).)
/E (Among the top 25 nickel mines  causing deforestation, many operate in regions classified as High Carbon Stock \(HCS\) forests. Six of these mines contain over 75% HCS forests, which are rich in carbon and intact ecosystems, while 14 consist of 50% or less, indicating degraded lands \(Brown & Harris, 2024\). These areas, which store significant amounts of carbon, face substantial risks due to nickel-driven deforestation, potentially releasing millions of metric tons of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. For instance, deforestation within nickel concessions on Halmahera alone has resulted in an estimated loss of 2.04 million metric tons of stored carbon \(CRI, 2024\).  Preliminary findings suggest that regions with high GDP growth driven by mining activities may exhibit a significant positive forest carbon flux, indicating a reduced capacity of forests to absorb carbon \(Figure 2.18\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
