1923 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The increasing proportion of informal employment in the region presents a paradox, given the introduction of extensive manufacturing activities. Theoretically, the arrival of new manufacturing firms is expected to create formal job opportunities, thereby transitioning the regional employment landscape from one dominated by informality to a structure characterized by more formal employment. This anomaly may be indicative of two potential scenarios. First, a reallocation of local labor from agriculture to non-agricultural sectors could be occurring, driven by externalities associated with the entry of new mining and manufacturing enterprises that adversely affect agricultural productivity, compelling workers to seek alternative employment. Moreover, nickel mining and manufacturing tend to attract significant in-migration, leading to increased demand for goods and services that displaced agricultural workers might supply through informal employment in non-agricultural sectors.)
/E (The increasing proportion of informal employment in the region presents a paradox, given the introduction of extensive manufacturing activities. Theoretically, the arrival of new manufacturing firms is expected to create formal job opportunities, thereby transitioning the regional employment landscape from one dominated by informality to a structure characterized by more formal employment. This anomaly may be indicative of two potential scenarios. First, a reallocation of local labor from agriculture to non-agricultural sectors could be occurring, driven by externalities associated with the entry of new mining and manufacturing enterprises that adversely affect agricultural productivity, compelling workers to seek alternative employment. Moreover, nickel mining and manufacturing tend to attract significant in-migration, leading to increased demand for goods and services that displaced agricultural workers might supply through informal employment in non-agricultural sectors.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 51 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1924 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Furthermore, the potential economic benefits derived from the consumption patterns of new residents may incentivize previously economically inactive individuals to enter the labor market as participants in the informal non-agricultural sector. Consequently, it can be inferred that the introduction of nickel mining and manufacturing may concurrently reduce employment in agriculture while fostering novel opportunities within the informal non-agricultural domain. Recent empirical evidence supports this hypothesis; for instance, Tritto \(2022\) reported that IMIP's industrial complex directly employed approximately 43,000 workers and indirectly supported around 30,000 small service providers and local businesses, which are pivotal in labor-intensive, informal economic activities in Indonesia.)
/E (Furthermore, the potential economic benefits derived from the consumption patterns of new residents may incentivize previously economically inactive individuals to enter the labor market as participants in the informal non-agricultural sector. Consequently, it can be inferred that the introduction of nickel mining and manufacturing may concurrently reduce employment in agriculture while fostering novel opportunities within the informal non-agricultural domain. Recent empirical evidence supports this hypothesis; for instance, Tritto \(2022\) reported that IMIP's industrial complex directly employed approximately 43,000 workers and indirectly supported around 30,000 small service providers and local businesses, which are pivotal in labor-intensive, informal economic activities in Indonesia.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 51 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
