1993 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Regarding economic factors, further investigation is warranted to ascertain the equitable distribution of benefits derived from nickel mining and processing. Our preliminary findings indicate an imbalance, as regional growth predominantly fuels manufacturing and informal employment sectors without fostering a transition towards more formalized employment structures. This observation is particularly salient given the government's frequent reliance on job creation as a primary justification for promoting these activities. While manufacturing employment is used as a proxy for formal employment, future research should aim to disaggregate employment data into formal and informal sectors for a more nuanced analysis.)
/E (Regarding economic factors, further investigation is warranted to ascertain the equitable distribution of benefits derived from nickel mining and processing. Our preliminary findings indicate an imbalance, as regional growth predominantly fuels manufacturing and informal employment sectors without fostering a transition towards more formalized employment structures. This observation is particularly salient given the government's frequent reliance on job creation as a primary justification for promoting these activities. While manufacturing employment is used as a proxy for formal employment, future research should aim to disaggregate employment data into formal and informal sectors for a more nuanced analysis.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1994 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Given the projected depletion of nickel reserves in Indonesia, the temporal constraints on realizing the full potential of nickel mining and processing are critical. We contend that current practices are not conducive to long-term sustainability, potentially leading to a negative net welfare effect. Consequently, substantive policy reforms are imperative to optimize the benefits derived from nickel mining and processing activities and to ensure a more sustainable trajectory.)
/E (Given the projected depletion of nickel reserves in Indonesia, the temporal constraints on realizing the full potential of nickel mining and processing are critical. We contend that current practices are not conducive to long-term sustainability, potentially leading to a negative net welfare effect. Consequently, substantive policy reforms are imperative to optimize the benefits derived from nickel mining and processing activities and to ensure a more sustainable trajectory.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [11]
>>
