2048 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Government Regulation No. 5/2021 on Risk-Based Business Licensing indicates an over-centralization of business licensing. Although local governments, through the Investment and One-Stop Service Agency \(DPMPTSP\), also have the authority to grant licenses, this authority is removed for foreign investments, as stated in Article 22. This is particularly concerning for the nickel downstream industry, as approximately 90% of nickel smelting operations are foreign-invested \(Kemenko Marves, 2024\).)
/E (Government Regulation No. 5/2021 on Risk-Based Business Licensing indicates an over-centralization of business licensing. Although local governments, through the Investment and One-Stop Service Agency \(DPMPTSP\), also have the authority to grant licenses, this authority is removed for foreign investments, as stated in Article 22. This is particularly concerning for the nickel downstream industry, as approximately 90% of nickel smelting operations are foreign-invested \(Kemenko Marves, 2024\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2049 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The regulation also centralizes risk assessments at the ministerial level in Article 19, excluding local governments from the decision-making process. Additionally, Article 21 establishes that the norms, standards, procedures, and criteria \(NSPK\) for risk-based business licensing are determined solely by the central government. This one-size-fits-all approach ignores the geographical and socio-environmental factors that affect the risks associated with specific industries. Moreover, the Law can serve as a shield for businesses, allowing them to proceed with construction even if they have not yet met full compliance with environmental assessments, with regulatory requirements deferred to later stages, as seen in the case of PT KFI \(Ardan, 2024\). )
/E (The regulation also centralizes risk assessments at the ministerial level in Article 19, excluding local governments from the decision-making process. Additionally, Article 21 establishes that the norms, standards, procedures, and criteria \(NSPK\) for risk-based business licensing are determined solely by the central government. This one-size-fits-all approach ignores the geographical and socio-environmental factors that affect the risks associated with specific industries. Moreover, the Law can serve as a shield for businesses, allowing them to proceed with construction even if they have not yet met full compliance with environmental assessments, with regulatory requirements deferred to later stages, as seen in the case of PT KFI \(Ardan, 2024\). )
/Alt ()
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [5 6]
