2060 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The Omnibus Law has removed Article 26\(2\) of Law No. 32/2009 \(UU PPLH\), which previously mandated that public participation in the preparation of Environmental Impact Assessments \(AMDAL\) be conducted with transparent and comprehensive information provided before project implementation. Additionally, Article 26\(4\), which granted the public the right to object to AMDAL documents, has also been eliminated. While other legal principles, such as the general legal right to file a lawsuit if harmed, Law No. 14/2008 on Public Information Disclosure, or the authority of environmental NGOs to file lawsuits under Article 92 of  Law No.32/2009, may still apply, this removal signals a further weakening of environmental accountability and limits community oversight on potentially harmful projects. )
/E (The Omnibus Law has removed Article 26\(2\) of Law No. 32/2009 \(UU PPLH\), which previously mandated that public participation in the preparation of Environmental Impact Assessments \(AMDAL\) be conducted with transparent and comprehensive information provided before project implementation. Additionally, Article 26\(4\), which granted the public the right to object to AMDAL documents, has also been eliminated. While other legal principles, such as the general legal right to file a lawsuit if harmed, Law No. 14/2008 on Public Information Disclosure, or the authority of environmental NGOs to file lawsuits under Article 92 of  Law No.32/2009, may still apply, this removal signals a further weakening of environmental accountability and limits community oversight on potentially harmful projects. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 63 0 R
/K [7 8 9]
>>
endobj
2061 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (This reduction of transparency is alarming, especially since the public disclosure has been a persistent issue for the Commission on Information, particularly in the mining sector. Between 2017 and 2022, 88% of the 25 information disputes related to AMDAL, Environmental Approval, or IUP involved requests for mining sector AMDAL documents, underscoring  the growing lack of transparency in environmental governance. Many institutions still consider licensing documents and contracts in the natural resources and environmental sectors as non-public and exempt from transparency regulations \(Thea, 2023\).)
/E (This reduction of transparency is alarming, especially since the public disclosure has been a persistent issue for the Commission on Information, particularly in the mining sector. Between 2017 and 2022, 88% of the 25 information disputes related to AMDAL, Environmental Approval, or IUP involved requests for mining sector AMDAL documents, underscoring  the growing lack of transparency in environmental governance. Many institutions still consider licensing documents and contracts in the natural resources and environmental sectors as non-public and exempt from transparency regulations \(Thea, 2023\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 63 0 R
/K [10 11]
>>
