2072 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The Job Creation Law has weakened criminal accountability for environmental violations by prioritizing administrative sanctions over criminal penalties. Key provisions in Law No. 32/2009 \(Articles 98, 99, 102, 104, 109, and 110\) have been amended to replace imprisonment with fines, making it more difficult  to prosecute corporate offenses. By emphasizing fines over imprisonment, enforcement disproportionately favors businesses with financial resources, allowing wealthier violators to avoid prosecution while only those unable to pay face legal consequences. This misinterpretation of the ultimum remedium principle weakens deterrence, as penalties are based on financial capacity rather than environmental harm, effectively reducing corporate responsibility \(FH UI, 2024\). )
/E (The Job Creation Law has weakened criminal accountability for environmental violations by prioritizing administrative sanctions over criminal penalties. Key provisions in Law No. 32/2009 \(Articles 98, 99, 102, 104, 109, and 110\) have been amended to replace imprisonment with fines, making it more difficult  to prosecute corporate offenses. By emphasizing fines over imprisonment, enforcement disproportionately favors businesses with financial resources, allowing wealthier violators to avoid prosecution while only those unable to pay face legal consequences. This misinterpretation of the ultimum remedium principle weakens deterrence, as penalties are based on financial capacity rather than environmental harm, effectively reducing corporate responsibility \(FH UI, 2024\). )
/Alt ()
/Pg 64 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
2073 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The enactment of Law No. 3/2020 on the Amendment to the Mineral and Coal Mining Law has centralized permitting authority and environmental assessments under the central government, similar to the Omnibus Law and GR No. 22/2021. This shift means that local governments no longer have jurisdiction to take action if communities are harmed by mining companies. Alarmingly, communities opposing mining activities could face criminal charges with fines of up to IDR100 million, further suppressing grassroots resistance, as outlined in Article 162. This stands in stark contrast to Article 66 of Law No. 32/2009, which protects the public fighting for environmental rights from criminalization.)
/E (The enactment of Law No. 3/2020 on the Amendment to the Mineral and Coal Mining Law has centralized permitting authority and environmental assessments under the central government, similar to the Omnibus Law and GR No. 22/2021. This shift means that local governments no longer have jurisdiction to take action if communities are harmed by mining companies. Alarmingly, communities opposing mining activities could face criminal charges with fines of up to IDR100 million, further suppressing grassroots resistance, as outlined in Article 162. This stands in stark contrast to Article 66 of Law No. 32/2009, which protects the public fighting for environmental rights from criminalization.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 64 0 R
/K [8]
>>
