2083 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The enactment of Law No. 6/2023 on Job Creation and its derivative regulations has significantly weakened forest protection, accelerating deforestation, especially due to nickel downstream activities. Under Law No. 41/1999 \( Article 38\), the use of forest areas for mining required a borrow-to-use permit granted by the Minister, and if the impact was significant, widespread, and strategically valuable, approval from the DPR \(Indonesian Parliament\) was also required. This is further reflected in Article 19, which states that any changes to forest designation that have significant impacts and strategic value require DPR approval. This process ensured legislative oversight for changes to forest designation or function. However, Article 36 of the Omnibus Law amends these two articles, removing the DPR approval clause. Additionally, while Article 19 of Law No. 41/1999 stated that changes to forest designation could be determined by the government, the Omnibus Law limits this authority exclusively to the central government. )
/E (The enactment of Law No. 6/2023 on Job Creation and its derivative regulations has significantly weakened forest protection, accelerating deforestation, especially due to nickel downstream activities. Under Law No. 41/1999 \( Article 38\), the use of forest areas for mining required a borrow-to-use permit granted by the Minister, and if the impact was significant, widespread, and strategically valuable, approval from the DPR \(Indonesian Parliament\) was also required. This is further reflected in Article 19, which states that any changes to forest designation that have significant impacts and strategic value require DPR approval. This process ensured legislative oversight for changes to forest designation or function. However, Article 36 of the Omnibus Law amends these two articles, removing the DPR approval clause. Additionally, while Article 19 of Law No. 41/1999 stated that changes to forest designation could be determined by the government, the Omnibus Law limits this authority exclusively to the central government. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [7 8]
>>
endobj
2084 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Under Law No. 41/1999 on Forestry, the use of forest areas for protected forests is categorized into three types under Article 26, while production forests are categorized into six types under Article 28, based on their function and purpose. However, the Omnibus Law simplifies this into a single "business permit", which streamlines business processes but risks oversimplifying the permits for various activities with different objectives. Furthermore, this business permit expands its beneficiaries to include not only individuals and cooperatives, as stipulated in Articles 27 and 29 of Law No. 41/1999, but also state-owned enterprises \(Badan Usaha Milik Negara/BUMN\), regional state-owned enterprises \(Badan Usaha Milik Daerah/BUMD\), and private companies. This deregulation favors businesses with capital and influence, making it significantly easier for large corporations to obtain forest concessions. )
/E (Under Law No. 41/1999 on Forestry, the use of forest areas for protected forests is categorized into three types under Article 26, while production forests are categorized into six types under Article 28, based on their function and purpose. However, the Omnibus Law simplifies this into a single "business permit", which streamlines business processes but risks oversimplifying the permits for various activities with different objectives. Furthermore, this business permit expands its beneficiaries to include not only individuals and cooperatives, as stipulated in Articles 27 and 29 of Law No. 41/1999, but also state-owned enterprises \(Badan Usaha Milik Negara/BUMN\), regional state-owned enterprises \(Badan Usaha Milik Daerah/BUMD\), and private companies. This deregulation favors businesses with capital and influence, making it significantly easier for large corporations to obtain forest concessions. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [9 10 11]
>>
