/T (Despite the significant risks, Indonesia continues to permit deep-sea tailings placement \(DSTP\) under GR No. 22/2021 and MoEF Regulation No. 6/2021. While Article 200 of the MoEF Regulation and Article 246 of the GR impose restrictions, including the requirement for technical approval and adherence to designated disposal locations, they also mandate the avoidance of sensitive marine areas such as coral reefs, mangrove forests, and fish spawning zones. Article 395 of the GR No.22/2021 outlines specific criteria for dumping sites, including the need for them to be located at a permanent thermocline on the ocean floor, or, if absent, at depths of 100 meters or more, with topography that directs tailings to 200-meter depths and no upwelling phenomena. Additionally, Article 391 imposes strict requirements for dumping B3 waste, mandating neutralization or detoxification of hazardous substances before disposal.)
/E (Despite the significant risks, Indonesia continues to permit deep-sea tailings placement \(DSTP\) under GR No. 22/2021 and MoEF Regulation No. 6/2021. While Article 200 of the MoEF Regulation and Article 246 of the GR impose restrictions, including the requirement for technical approval and adherence to designated disposal locations, they also mandate the avoidance of sensitive marine areas such as coral reefs, mangrove forests, and fish spawning zones. Article 395 of the GR No.22/2021 outlines specific criteria for dumping sites, including the need for them to be located at a permanent thermocline on the ocean floor, or, if absent, at depths of 100 meters or more, with topography that directs tailings to 200-meter depths and no upwelling phenomena. Additionally, Article 391 imposes strict requirements for dumping B3 waste, mandating neutralization or detoxification of hazardous substances before disposal.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 68 0 R
/K [8]
